#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176793} :activity/id #uuid "bf0ecc39-1fe8-504f-aa46-f80a00074638" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049325 :topic/id #uuid "ec0cc1a2-4f65-5bb2-b5ae-116b4850cf11" :topic/math-academy-id 3671} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177028 :tutorial/content "Recall that Euler's number can be defined as the following special limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{m \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{m}\\right)^{m}\n$$\n\nWe can derive an alternative version of this limit by making the substitution\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac 1m\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that if $m\\to \\infty$ then $n \\to 0$. Therefore, applying our substitution, we arrive at the following important result:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+{n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "490157c8-33d8-5f0d-ad6e-f837c839499f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef158b4c-de98-5033-958d-aa50b93d6909" :step/math-academy-id 35058 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176018 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175873 :question/id #uuid "1cc5d11a-c497-5d8f-84b4-9cbef561db28" :question/math-academy-id "e-9364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c8893eb-cc9c-59b1-98c8-ec326a352e28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049769}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176308}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using the Alternative Limit Definition of Euler's Number"} :step/id #uuid "ac8ee34c-7875-5657-81c3-a58946bcf638" :step/math-academy-id 35060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176019 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175874 :question/id #uuid "caad75a3-4ee4-5038-b9e1-5e296d83974a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9366"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dbad5d4-6001-5be5-9ed3-0b959f4d7d92" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176302}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "797a10b6-090b-55e9-8c36-939dfb954adc" :step/math-academy-id 35066 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176801}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177029 :tutorial/content "Let's take stock of our two special limit definitions of Euler's number:\n\n- $\\displaystyle e = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^{n}$\n- $\\displaystyle e = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{1/n}$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate more complex limits. For example, suppose we wish to evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{x+3}\\right)^{x+3}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $x \\to \\infty$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{x+3}\\right)^{x+3}} = 1^\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)^n = e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x+3}=\\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x + 3} &= \\frac{1}{n} \\\\\nx + 3 &= 2n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)(1 + \\frac{2}{x + 3})^{x + 3} &= lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{2n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)[(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n})^{n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39210b04-2951-5b70-adbb-7a6690671dac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5710 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit That Resembles a Special Limit Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "129e19b0-cbae-5677-bfcb-93c996ee597b" :step/math-academy-id 35067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176022 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175877 :question/id #uuid "63c2e3b3-3418-5f74-8ebc-34af9fd6bf56" :question/math-academy-id "e-9377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9308a83f-ed86-5cbc-9b23-c1fec9fca6a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049760}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503687}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit Using a Variable Substitution: Advanced Cases"} :step/id #uuid "88bbb3ae-6920-5592-b9b2-5833516142b8" :step/math-academy-id 35109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177032 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(1-\\sin{x}\\right)^{3/\\sin{x}}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $x \\to 0$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left(1-\\sin{x}\\right)^{3/\\sin{x}} = 1^{\\infty}\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{1/n} = e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\sin x)^{3/\\sin x} &= lim_(x → 0)(1 + (-\\sin x))^{3/\\sin x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $-\\sin{x}=n$. Therefore, since $n \\to 0$ as $x \\to 0$, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\sin x)^{3/\\sin x} &= lim_(x → 0)(1 + (-\\sin x))^{3/\\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{3/(-n)} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)[(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{-3} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{-3} \\\\\n&= e^{-3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{e^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fdccc8f6-9726-5fd5-846c-74c2c5b1ed28" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5717 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit Containing a Minus Sign Using a Variable Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "68a5d41c-4aef-5d14-b412-4042829e22c7" :step/math-academy-id 35113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177035 :tutorial/content "We have shown that Euler's number $e$ is given by the following limit:\n\n$$\ne = \\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+{n}\\right)^{1/n}\n$$\n\nLet's use this result to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{2/n}}\n$$\n\nNotice that as $n \\to 0$,\n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1$, while\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty$.\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{\\lim_{n \\to 0} \\left(1+n\\right)^{2/n}} = 1^\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nHowever, by rewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and then applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{2/n} &= lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{2 \\cdot 1/n} \\\\\n&= lim_(n → 0)[(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= [lim_(n → 0)(1 + n)^{1/n}]^{2} \\\\\n&= e^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cde764e2-c036-59de-af59-f13454800e69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5722 :tutorial/title "Evaluating a Limit Using the Alternative Limit Definition of Euler's Number"} :step/id #uuid "1a2ed7d1-2776-5a8c-9da9-bd1e0c7a4cdc" :step/math-academy-id 35122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176794}}] :activity/title "Further Limits Involving the Exponential Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176173}]}
